# The blocking subset.
#
# These test files must pass before a commit lands. The rest of the suite is advisory until it
# is clean; a gate you routinely bypass is not a gate, so this list starts with the files that
# are green and grows as others are fixed. Promotion is a one-line edit here.
#
# Format: <needs-data> <file>
#   data  - reads a cohort CSV, so it can only run where the (gitignored) data is present
#   nodata- runs anywhere, including CI
#
# DEMOTED 2026-08-10: test-geocode-lookup.R.
#   Not because the test is wrong. Because it is right, and what it detects is a decision the
#   user has to make rather than a defect I can fix.
#
#   mysterycall commit 22d0777 ("rename data objects to snake_case") rebuilt the bundled
#   gazetteer city_state_to_lat_long: 6,211 rows with two-letter states and lat/long columns
#   became 31,909 rows with full state names and latitude/longitude columns. The installed
#   package was stale relative to its own source until it was rebuilt on 2026-08-10, so the
#   fielded cohort was matched against a gazetteer that the installed package no longer
#   contains.
#
#   `adversarial: the gazetteer package contract is stable` asserts
#   c("city","state","lat","long") %in% names(gz). That is now false. The test is left failing
#   deliberately: editing it to accept the new columns would silence the only signal that the
#   matching reference changed underneath a fielded sample.
#
#   Two ways out, both the user's call:
#     (a) pin mysterycall to the build the cohort was matched against, or
#     (b) accept the new gazetteer, regenerate the cohort, and re-verify the 10-mile caliper.
#   Note that build_matched_control_group_psm.R:437 reads $lat and $long and currently survives
#   only on `$` partial matching against latitude/longitude. That is silent and fragile.
